# Release Tracker Migration Planning
## Migration from Java Spring Boot to Python within AFO (AADLC)

# Objective

The existing Release Compliance Tracker has reached a mature state and contains significant business logic that should be preserved.

The objective is NOT to merge this application into the existing AFO Release Activities pages.

Instead, create a brand-new tool inside the existing AFO Tools section called:

# Release Tracker

This tool will become another first-class tool inside AFO, similar to the other existing tools.

The complete Release Tracker application should remain functionally independent while leveraging the common AFO platform capabilities (authentication, hosting, database standards, etc.).

---

# IMPORTANT

Do NOT begin implementation immediately.

The first phase is architecture analysis and migration planning.

Only after the migration plan is reviewed should implementation begin.

---

# Final Target Architecture

## Frontend

Reuse the existing Release Tracker frontend almost as-is.

Technology

- React 18
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Tailwind CSS

The current frontend is already polished and production-quality.

Do NOT rewrite it into Streamlit.

Instead, integrate it into the AFO platform as a standalone React-based tool.

Existing UI should be reused wherever possible.

Existing CSS should remain unchanged unless integration requires small adjustments.

---

## Backend

Completely migrate backend from Java Spring Boot to Python.

Current

- Java 17
- Spring Boot 3.x

Target

Python backend following existing AFO project conventions.

Business logic should remain identical.

Only language/framework changes are expected.

---

## Database

Reuse the same database strategy already used by AFO.

Development

SQLite

Production

PostgreSQL (existing AFO standard)

Reuse existing database access patterns wherever possible.

Migrate all Release Tracker tables into the Python database layer.

Do not redesign the data model unless absolutely necessary.

---

## Authentication

Reuse existing Azure Entra ID authentication.

No separate login.

No duplicate authentication.

---

## Authorization

Every actionable UI button must validate user authorization.

Examples include:

- Generate Report
- Refresh Deployment Status
- Send Teams Notification
- Download Reports
- Settings Updates
- Any write operation

Developer role

- Read-only operations
- View dashboards
- View reports
- View readiness

Admin role

- Generate reports
- Refresh deployment
- Send Teams notifications
- Configuration changes
- Administrative operations

If AFO already contains appropriate authorization roles, reuse them.

Otherwise introduce

- Admin
- Developer

using Azure AD Groups.

